Z||000000||Seanh||10-03-2001||12:35 AM||||Can the TV cable be adjusted to give me firmer, more crisp shifts? If so, how do I do it? Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 4spd Auto, getting ready to install T-6 turbo.||||reg||1
Z||000001||MontanaFiero||10-03-2001||03:54 AM||||try tweeking the rabbit ears or wrapping some tin foil around the shift nob. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">:<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/blee291/fiero/mtfieroplate.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000002||SKIDMARK||10-03-2001||09:42 AM||||These transaxles are known for their spongy shifts. The best way to get crisp shifts etc. is to install a shift kit - about $35. The bad news is that you have to pull the trans to do it. The TV cable will only change the shift points depending on load, accelerator position, speed, etc. Properly adjusting the TV cable is not always easy. The shift kit comes with some good instructions for adjustment.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Seanh||10-03-2001||10:38 PM||||I am going to install a shift kit, but I doubt it will shift like I want it to still. I was thinking the TV cable might have somthing to do with firmness of the shift or somthing...Just wondering. <P>I'm pretty sure you don't have to pull our tranny to install the shift kit do ya? I'v never had to...We could be thinking of different shift kits too...<P>I want the tranny to shift brutally, just slam the thing into every gear as hard as possible. I don't find removing the engine and tranny at all difficult, so if it does wear the tranny faster I don't care. I want to build a tranny for my car, but I'm haveing a great deal of trouble finding anyone who sells heavy duty parts for our tranny, but have had some success finding some for the 4spd autos like thats in my car now, so I'm going to locate one of them and build it in my garage. All I'm really hopping for now is it to chirp the tires into 2nd, and I'd be happy for a while...||||reg||1
Z||000004||Mach10||10-04-2001||10:44 PM||||I KNOW that you can control shift-quality with the KD cable. I've done it with my Jag. Thing is, I have NO idea how to do it with a Fiero. With the Jag, you need a pressure gauge onto one of the ports in the tranny, and keep adjusting the cable until the desired line pressure is reached.<BR>WARNING: You can REALLY FUXX0R a tranny like this, if not done properly. I know at least one forum member's done it, as they said they did. I hope he/she reads this and tells all  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
